---
title: SSH Keys
order: 2
description: ssh-keygen, ssh-agent, ssh-add, authorized_keys
tags: [ssh, keys, keygen, agent, ed25519, rsa, authorized_keys]
---

Key-based authentication is more secure than passwords. Ed25519 is the
recommended algorithm; RSA (4096-bit) is the fallback for legacy systems.

@code bash
# ---- ssh-keygen: generate keys ----

# ed25519 (recommended)
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "user@hostname"
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "user@hostname" -f ~/.ssh/id_myproject

# rsa (legacy compatibility)
ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096 -C "user@hostname"
ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096 -f ~/.ssh/id_legacy

# ecdsa (alternative)
ssh-keygen -t ecdsa -b 521 -C "user@hostname"

# generate without passphrase (automation only)
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/id_automation -N ""

@code bash
# key management
ssh-keygen -p -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519        # change passphrase
ssh-keygen -y -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519        # print public key from private
ssh-keygen -l -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub    # show fingerprint
ssh-keygen -l -E md5 -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub    # MD5 fingerprint
ssh-keygen -lv -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub   # visual fingerprint (randomart)

@code bash
# ---- deploy public key to remote host ----

# preferred method
ssh-copy-id user@hostname
ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/id_mykey.pub user@hostname       # specific key
ssh-copy-id -p 2222 user@hostname                       # custom port

# manual method
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub | ssh user@hostname 'mkdir -p ~/.ssh && chmod 700 ~/.ssh && cat >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys && chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys'

@text
authorized_keys format: one key per line. Each line can include options
to restrict what the key can do.

@code bash
# ---- ~/.ssh/authorized_keys ----

# basic entry (just the public key)
ssh-ed25519 AAAA...key... user@hostname

# with restrictions
command="/usr/bin/backup-script" ssh-ed25519 AAAA...    # force specific command
no-port-forwarding ssh-ed25519 AAAA...                   # disable port forwarding
no-pty ssh-ed25519 AAAA...                               # disable terminal
from="192.168.1.0/24" ssh-ed25519 AAAA...               # restrict source IP

# combined restrictions
from="10.0.0.*",no-port-forwarding,no-X11-forwarding,command="/usr/local/bin/backup" ssh-ed25519 AAAA...

@code bash
# ---- ssh-agent: key caching ----

# start agent (if not already running)
eval $(ssh-agent)

# or add to ~/.bashrc for persistent agent
if [ -z "$SSH_AUTH_SOCK" ]; then
    eval $(ssh-agent) > /dev/null
fi

# KDE Plasma / desktop environments often run an agent automatically
# check with:
echo $SSH_AUTH_SOCK
ssh-add -l                                # list loaded keys

@code bash
# ---- ssh-add: manage agent keys ----

ssh-add                                   # add default key (~/.ssh/id_*)
ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_myproject               # add specific key
ssh-add -l                                # list loaded keys (fingerprints)
ssh-add -L                                # list loaded keys (full public keys)
ssh-add -d ~/.ssh/id_myproject            # remove specific key
ssh-add -D                                # remove all keys
ssh-add -t 3600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519        # add with 1-hour lifetime

@text
Agent forwarding lets you use your local keys on a remote machine to
connect to a third machine. Use with caution: a compromised remote host
can use your agent while you are connected.

@code bash
# agent forwarding
ssh -A user@bastion                       # forward agent to bastion
# then from bastion:
ssh user@internal                         # uses forwarded key

# in ~/.ssh/config
Host bastion
    ForwardAgent yes

@text
File permissions matter. SSH refuses to use keys with permissive access:

@code bash
# required permissions
chmod 700 ~/.ssh                          # directory
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519              # private key
chmod 644 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub          # public key
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys         # authorized keys
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/config                  # config file
chmod 644 ~/.ssh/known_hosts             # known hosts

# check and fix
ls -la ~/.ssh/
